    "original_title": "Ethiopia-style guerrilla insurgencies could emerge in Somalia: official",
    "slug": "former-intelligence-chief-warns-of-ethiopia-style-insurgencies-in-somalia",
    "summary": "Abdirahman Turyare, Somalia's envoy to the Islamic Military Counter-Terrorism Coalition, cautioned that political rivalries could lead to guerrilla factions similar to those in Ethiopia's Tigray, Amhara, and Oromia regions, specifically in Hiran, Middle Shabelle, and Bay.",
    "content": "MOGADISHU, Somalia — Abdirahman Turyare, a former head of Somali intelligence and current Somali envoy to the Islamic Military Counter-Terrorism Coalition, warned on Friday that Somalia may witness the rise of guerrilla insurgencies akin to those in Ethiopia's Tigray, Amhara, and Oromia regions. Speaking at a public event in Mogadishu, Turyare attributed the risk to deepening political rivalries and the reported pursuit of military support by competing power centers.\n\nTuryare specifically identified the regions of Hiran, Middle Shabelle, and Bay as potential hotspots for such armed movements. He did not provide detailed evidence but emphasized that the proliferation of armed groups could destabilize these areas further. His warning comes amid heightened political tensions in Somalia, where federal and regional authorities have clashed over electoral processes and resource allocation, raising fears of broader conflict.\n\nThe former intelligence chief's remarks also follow recent reports of informal militias being formed by various political actors. Turyare's experience includes leading the National Intelligence and Security Agency (NISA) from 2013 to 2018, during which he oversaw counterterrorism operations against al-Shabaab. His current role involves coordinating with the Saudi-led Islamic Military Counter-Terrorism Coalition, giving him insight into regional security dynamics.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, these developments are particularly concerning. Many diaspora members maintain close ties with family and investments in Hiran, Middle Shabelle, and Bay, and any escalation of violence could lead to displacement and economic hardship. Additionally, the diaspora has been actively involved in peacebuilding and development initiatives in these regions, and the emergence of guerrilla factions could undermine those efforts.\n\nAnalysts note that Somalia's political landscape has been fraught with tensions between the federal government and regional states, particularly Jubbaland and Puntland, over issues such as elections and constitutional review. The proliferation of armed groups could further fragment the country's security landscape, complicating efforts to combat al-Shabaab and stabilize the region.\n\nTuryare's warning underscores the need for dialogue and reconciliation among Somali political stakeholders to prevent the country from descending into wider conflict. The international community, including diaspora organizations, may need to intensify mediation efforts to address the underlying grievances driving political rivalries.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/ethiopia-style-guerrilla-insurgencies-somalia/",
